Description: wlr-screencopy: take capture_output_region's region as output-local
 The protocol gives the region of capture_output_region in the output's
 logical coordinate space (0,0 is the output's top left), and clients such
 as wf-recorder subtract the output's position before sending it. Mir
 clipped the region against the output's extents in layout coordinates, so
 on any output not at the layout's origin the region was shifted back by
 the output's position and mostly clipped away: recording a 3440x1440
 monitor placed right of a 1920 px wide one gave a 1520x1440 video of its
 left part. Move the region onto the output before clipping.
Author: Umoja developers
Forwarded: not yet
Last-Update: 2026-11-10
---
--- a/src/server/frontend_wayland/wlr_screencopy_v1.cpp
+++ b/src/server/frontend_wayland/wlr_screencopy_v1.cpp
@@ -393,7 +393,10 @@
 {
     auto const& output_config = OutputGlobal::from_or_throw(output).current_config();
     auto const extents = output_config.extents();
-    auto const intersection = intersection_of({{x, y}, {width, height}}, extents);
+    // The region is in the output's own logical coordinates (its top left
+    // is 0,0), not the layout's: move it onto the output before clipping.
+    auto const region = geom::Rectangle{extents.top_left + geom::Displacement{x, y}, {width, height}};
+    auto const intersection = intersection_of(region, extents);
     auto const buffer_size = translate_and_scale(intersection, extents, {{}, extents.size}).size;
     new WlrScreencopyFrameV1{frame, this, ctx, {output, intersection, buffer_size, output_config.transformation(), overlay_cursor == 1}};
 }
